## T2 â€” Server attempt and sender fencing with key correlation
Reasoning: Every outbox guarantee rests on a server contract that does not exist yet; it must be proven at integration 
level, including old-client compatibility, before any client queue relies on it.
Task: Keyed creates may carry an attempt generation and an expected sender; with them, a create commits only when its 
generation is current for (sender, conversation, key) at commit time, and a sender mismatch is rejected without 
storing. A new fence/lookup route, for the caller's own key only, atomically returns the stored message or makes every 
lower generation unable to commit, rejects older generations, never creates a message, and rejects anon and other 
users' keys. The original submitted composition is stored for replay comparison. Create and fence responses carry 
`clientMessageId`; outcome codes follow BR-005. Compatibility: unkeyed creates behave as today; keyed requests without 
a generation (Phase 4 client) keep the Phase 4 keyed contract for text, attachment, and voice, including replay and 
409, and commit only if no fence state exists for the key at commit time (Standing rules, D4); otherwise they store 
nothing and are rejected as stale.
Done when: AC-044 (server part), AC-045 cases (a)â€“(c), and the AC-046 status codes hold against local Postgres and 
the real routes with at most one stored message per key, and Phase 4 keyed and legacy unkeyed requests still behave as 
before.
Verify by: New messaging DB integration tests for AC-044 (crafted mismatched sender, anon fence, other-user key), 
AC-045 (row-lock-blocked insert, held stale request, stale fence), AC-046 codes, generation-less requests losing to a 
fence (held request and row-lock-blocked insert, nothing stored afterwards), and compatibility (unkeyed text; Phase 4 
keyed text, attachment, voice; replays of their stored messages and 409) pass; RLS review gate recorded.
Covers: FR-006, FR-011, FR-034, BR-001, BR-004, BR-005, AC-044, AC-045, AC-046, AC-035
Root: T2
Depends on: â€”

## T3 â€” Fail-closed create and fence budgets with winning-insert quota
Reasoning: D1 makes replays quota-free, which needs separate, non-bypassable budgets; the current limiter is fail-open 
and counts attempts, not insertions.
Task: The insertion quota is charged in the same transaction as a winning insertion (once among concurrent same-key 
requests; never more stored messages than the quota with distinct keys at the boundary; not charged for replays); 
every authenticated create request â€” including malformed bodies, early-validation failures, replays, and quota 
losers â€” consumes a per-user request budget whose charge survives an insertion rollback; fence/lookup calls consume 
their own per-user budget; any exhausted budget returns 429; a budget that cannot be checked refuses the request as 
429 (D4 planning decision).
Done when: The AC-009 route-level clauses hold on the local database, including under a real limiter 
permission/availability failure.
Verify by: Messaging DB integration tests for each AC-009 route clause plus malformed-body and early-validation 
charging, rollback survival, and a limiter failure (revoked/unavailable function) producing 429; Phase 4/legacy 
compatibility tests pass; RLS review gate recorded.
Covers: FR-008, AC-009, AC-035
Root: T3
Depends on: T2

